## Task

Write a FULL component documentation page — not a one-paragraph blurb. Real UI-kit docs (Radix
Primitives, Ark UI — this kit's own upstream) converge on the same shape; match it, grounding every
sentence in the data above:

1. **Overview** — one short paragraph: what this component is for, in plain language grounded in
   `editorInfo.variantAxis`/`group`/the part meanings — not marketing copy.

2. **Features** — a bullet list of concrete capabilities, each traceable to a real setting, state,
   or part in the data above. Don't pad with generic claims ("accessible", "customizable") that
   aren't grounded in something here — every bullet should read as a fact you could point at in the
   JSON.

3. **Anatomy** — a short JSX *skeleton* (imports + nesting, no real content) showing how the real
   parts nest, using the exported names from the component source and the nesting rules in
   `editorInfo.parts[name].accepts`. This differs from the flat anatomy TABLE already in the
   generated file (part → meaning, no shape) — the skeleton's job is to show composition, which a
   flat table can't.

4. **Examples** — several, each a short paragraph plus a complete, real code snippet using the
   actual exported names/props from the component source. Derive the SET of examples from what's
   actually in the data: one example per setting worth demonstrating (showing its effect), one for
   a state worth showing (e.g. a disabled item), one composing something inside a part whose
   `accepts` allows arbitrary content/components if that's a realistic use. Skip an example that
   doesn't correspond to something in the data — don't invent scenarios to pad the count.

5. **Styling hooks** — one short paragraph: every state/setting above carrying a `mark` (not `—`)
   is a real CSS hook a skin can select on (`[data-state="open"]` etc., see this file's "The wider
   framework" section above). Don't repeat the marks table — just orient the reader that these are
   the intended styling surface.

6. **Accessibility** — the ONE section where you may draw on general, well-known knowledge beyond
   the data given: the WAI-ARIA Authoring Practices Guide pattern this component follows, inferred
   from `editorInfo.group`/`genus`/the part names (an accordion follows APG's "Accordion" pattern,
   a menu follows "Menu or Menu bar", etc.). State that pattern's standard keyboard interactions as
   a table (Key | What it does). If you're not confident which APG pattern applies, say so plainly
   instead of inventing key bindings — a missing table is honest, a wrong one is not.

Put the whole result inside the `## Notes` section of `src/{{name}}/README.md`, between the
`<!-- user:start -->` / `<!-- user:end -->` markers — that content survives the next mechanical
regeneration (`pnpm run readme`); everything else in that file does not. Everything else in that
file (title, anatomy/states/settings/CSS-variables tables) is already generated mechanically from
the same passport/editorInfo data — don't restate those tables, write what they can't carry.
